# DELIVERY HEADERS. Cloudflare Workers static assets parses this file out of
# the assets directory rather than serving it, the same way Pages does.
#
# ONE THING IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT: there is no `Cache-Control` on the HTML,
# and in particular no `no-transform`. That header is what stops Cloudflare
# injecting the Web Analytics beacon, and the injection is how analytics is
# wired here -- no snippet in the repository, one switch in the dashboard. A
# cache directive typed onto `/*` for tidiness would turn analytics off
# silently, with no error anywhere to find it by.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  # Nothing here is meant to be framed, and a marketing page inside somebody
  # else's frame is a clickjacking surface for no gain.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  # The site asks for none of these. It is a dictation product's page, not the
  # product: no `getUserMedia` anywhere in `src/`, checked rather than assumed.
  Permissions-Policy: microphone=(), camera=(), geolocation=()

# THE LEGAL ROUTES THIS SITE STILL SERVES, AT THE TRANSPORT LAYER AS WELL AS
# IN THE HEAD. Two of them: the imprint moved to legal.sw-labs.de and is a link
# from here rather than a page (ADR 0265), so `/imprint/` needs a redirect in
# ./_redirects and no rule in this file.
#
# `src/layouts/Legal.astro` already emits `<meta name="robots" content="noindex,
# follow">` on all three, and that is the authoritative copy: it is generated
# from the layout every legal document renders through, so a fourth route
# inherits it without anybody remembering. ADR 0264 carries why they are off
# the index at all.
#
# THIS IS THE SECOND COPY AND IT IS DELIBERATE. Neither page prints an address
# any more, so the stake is lower than it was -- but a `noindex` that lives
# only in the markup is still one accidental layout edit away from gone, and
# the failure is silent: the page keeps rendering, the build keeps passing, and
# a legal document is back in a listing weeks later. The header is read from
# the response rather than from the document, so both would have to be lost
# together.
#
# THEY CANNOT DRIFT APART SILENTLY. `scripts/launch-check.mjs` reads the paths
# out of this file, reads `LEGAL_ROUTES` out of `src/lib/legal.ts`, and refuses
# to deploy unless the two sets are equal.
#
# ONE RULE PER ROUTE, THE BARE PATH, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A
# STYLE CHOICE. Both forms were written first, on the reasoning that a splat
# might not match an empty remainder and the redundant line cost nothing. It
# cost something: probed against `wrangler dev` on 2026-08-30, `/imprint/`
# matched BOTH rules and the response carried `X-Robots-Tag` twice. Combined
# directives are read as combined, so the duplicate was harmless and it was
# also noise in a response whose whole job is to be unambiguous. The splat is
# what got dropped: these routes have nothing under them, and the bare path is
# the exact URL that is served.
#
# `follow`, not `nofollow`: these pages reference the provider and the hosting
# vendor's own notice, and a crawler arriving on a stale link should still be
# able to take the way home.

/privacy/
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

/terms/
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow

# Content-addressed by the build. A year is the point of the hash.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Self-hosted faces, four of them, and they do not change without a new file
# name being chosen for them.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Not content-addressed: `sync-assets.mjs` copies these under stable names, so
# a refreshed logo has to be able to land. A week, not a year.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# THE PREVIEW HOST IS NOT HANDLED HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
#
# `<name>.<subdomain>.workers.dev` is a second public, indexable copy of this
# site. The obvious answer is an `X-Robots-Tag: noindex` on that host, and it is
# the wrong one twice: the syntax allows exactly one `*` per rule, so
# `https://*.workers.dev/*` cannot be written, and a header only asks a crawler
# not to list a page that is still being served to anyone who has the URL.
#
# It is closed in `wrangler.jsonc` instead, with `workers_dev: false` and
# `preview_urls: false`. The host does not exist. If either is ever turned back
# on, this file is where the noindex belongs, and the account's own subdomain
# has to be spelled out in it.
